            interviewQuestions: [
              'Why is a well-designed Modular Monolith often superior to premature Microservices for startups and entry-level engineering teams?',
              'How do asynchronous Message Queues (BullMQ, RabbitMQ, AWS SQS) decouple producers and consumers and smooth out sudden traffic spikes?',
              'Explain the Circuit Breaker Pattern (Closed, Open, Half-Open states) and how it prevents cascading outages across distributed services.',
              'What is the Transactional Outbox Pattern and how does it guarantee atomic database updates and message publishing without 2-phase commit distributed transactions?',
              'What is the difference between At-Least-Once, At-Most-Once, and Exactly-Once message delivery semantics, and why is consumer idempotency essential?',
              'What is a Dead Letter Queue (DLQ), and how do you configure exponential backoff retry policies for failing background workers?',
              'How does a Distributed Rate Limiter work using Redis: Token Bucket vs Leaky Bucket vs Sliding Window Counter algorithms?',
              'How do you design a database schema migration strategy (Expand and Contract / Blue-Green) for zero-downtime schema changes in production?'
            ],
            revisionNotes: [
              { title: 'Modular Monolith vs Microservices', text: 'Start with a Modular Monolith (clear module boundaries, single deployment artifact). Move to Microservices only when independent team deployment or specialized scaling requires it.' },
              { title: 'Asynchronous Message Queues', text: 'Queues (BullMQ, RabbitMQ, SQS) decouple producers from consumers, buffer traffic spikes, and execute slow operations (emails, PDF rendering) asynchronously in background.' },
              { title: 'Circuit Breaker Pattern', text: 'Closed (normal traffic) -> Open (trip on failure threshold; fail fast without calling downstream) -> Half-Open (send probe requests to test downstream health).' },
              { title: 'Transactional Outbox Pattern', text: 'Write business data and event message to the same database in a single ACID transaction. A background poller or CDC (Debezium) publishes outbox messages to Kafka/RabbitMQ.' },
              { title: 'Message Delivery Semantics', text: 'At-Least-Once (messages may be duplicated -> consumer MUST be idempotent). At-Most-Once (messages may be lost). Exactly-Once (expensive end-to-end coordination).' },
              { title: 'Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)', text: 'When a background job fails after max retries (e.g. 5 attempts with exponential backoff), route payload to a DLQ for manual inspection without crashing workers.' },
              { title: 'Zero-Downtime Schema Migrations', text: 'Expand and Contract pattern: 1. Add new nullable column. 2. Write to both old and new columns. 3. Backfill old rows. 4. Switch reads to new column. 5. Drop old column.' }
            ]
