            interviewQuestions: [
              "Explain the Vanishing and Exploding Gradient problems in deep neural networks. How do architectural innovations (Residual connections, LayerNorm) and activation functions (ReLU, GELU) mitigate them?",
              "Why is initializing weights with all zeros catastrophic in deep feed-forward networks, and how do Xavier (Glorot) and He (Kaiming) initializations maintain activation variance across layers?",
              "Explain the mathematical difference between Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) with Momentum, RMSprop, and AdamW. Why is decoupled weight decay in AdamW critical for generalization?",
              "What is the difference between Batch Normalization and Layer Normalization? Why is LayerNorm preferred in Transformer architectures and small-batch sequence models?",
              "Derive the derivative of the Softmax cross-entropy loss function with respect to logits (p_i - y_i). Why is this numerical formulation stable against overflow/underflow?",
              "How does Dropout act as an implicit ensemble technique during training, and how is it deactivated or scaled during inference?"
            ],
            revisionNotes: [
              { title: 'Vanishing vs Exploding Gradients', text: 'Vanishing gradients (sigmoid/tanh derivative <0.25) cause early layer weights to stop updating. Exploding gradients cause numerical overflow. Fixes: ReLU/GELU activations, He/Xavier weight init, LayerNorm, Residual skip connections, gradient clipping.' },
              { title: 'AdamW vs Adam (Decoupled Weight Decay)', text: 'Standard Adam applies L2 regularization to gradient moments, scaling weight decay by historical learning rates. AdamW decouples weight decay and subtracts it directly from weights, restoring proper generalization.' },
              { title: 'BatchNorm vs LayerNorm Rule', text: 'BatchNorm normalizes across the batch dimension (fails on small batches <8 and variable-length sequences). LayerNorm normalizes across feature dimensions per sample independently → required for Transformers and RNNs.' },
              { title: 'Weight Initialization Rule', text: 'Never initialize weights to zero (neurons become symmetric and compute identical gradients). Use He (Kaiming) normal init for ReLU/GELU layers; use Xavier (Glorot) init for Sigmoid/Tanh layers.' },
              { title: 'Dropout Execution Mechanics', text: 'During training, Dropout zeros out neuron activations with probability p and scales remaining outputs by 1/(1-p) (inverted dropout). During inference, Dropout is completely deactivated (model.eval()).' }
            ]
