            revisionNotes: [
              {
                title: '1. Images, Containers & VM Comparison',
                text: 'Image: Immutable snapshot containing application code, runtime, libraries, and config. Container: Running isolated instance of an image sharing host OS kernel. Unlike VMs (which run full guest OS on hypervisors), containers share the host kernel for near-zero overhead.',
              },
              {
                title: '2. Multi-Stage Dockerfile Optimization',
                text: 'Stage 1 (deps): Install npm packages. Stage 2 (builder): Compile TypeScript / Next.js assets. Stage 3 (runner): Copy only compiled dist/, production node_modules, and runtime binaries into a minimal Alpine base image (<100MB vs 1GB+ development image).',
              },
              {
                title: '3. Container Security: Non-Root Execution',
                text: 'By default, Docker runs processes as root inside containers. If a container breakout occurs, attackers gain host root privileges. Mitigation: Create and switch to non-root user (USER node) in Dockerfile before running the startup command.',
              },
              {
                title: '4. Docker Compose Multi-Container Orchestration',
                text: 'docker-compose.yml defines services, networks, and volumes. Use depends_on with condition: service_healthy to ensure backend starts only after PostgreSQL and Redis pass their internal pg_isready and redis-cli ping health checks.',
              },
              {
                title: '5. Docker Volumes & Persistent Storage',
                text: 'Containers are ephemeral; data written inside is lost upon container destruction. Named Volumes (postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data) persist DB data across restarts. Bind Mounts (./src:/app/src) sync host files for instant local hot reloading.',
              },
            ],
