            interviewQuestions: [
              'What is Data Leakage in Machine Learning? Give examples of target leakage and train-test contamination, and how to prevent them using Scikit-Learn Pipelines.',
              'Compare One-Hot Encoding, Ordinal Encoding, and Target (Mean) Encoding. When is Target Encoding dangerous, and how do you regularize it?',
              'How do you handle missing values when data is Missing Completely at Random (MCAR), Missing at Random (MAR), or Missing Not at Random (MNAR)?',
              'Explain StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler, and RobustScaler. Which scaler should you use when your dataset contains extreme outliers?',
              'How does SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) generate synthetic points, and why must SMOTE be applied ONLY on training folds during cross-validation?',
              'What is the purpose of polynomial features and interaction terms in linear models, and how do you prevent feature explosion?',
              'How do you build a modular ColumnTransformer that handles numerical, low-cardinality categorical, and high-cardinality categorical pipelines simultaneously?'
            ],
            revisionNotes: [
              { title: 'CONCEPT: Data Leakage', text: 'Data leakage occurs when information from outside the training dataset (or future target information) is used to create features or fit scalers/imputers. Always .fit() on train and .transform() on test.' },
              { title: 'KEY DIFFERENCE: One-Hot vs Target Encoding', text: 'One-Hot creates sparse binary columns (impractical for high cardinality >50); Target Encoding maps categories to mean target values but causes target leakage without out-of-fold regularization.' },
              { title: 'RULE: RobustScaler for Outliers', text: 'StandardScaler uses mean and standard deviation (distorted by outliers); RobustScaler subtracts the median and divides by IQR, preserving outlier robustness.' },
              { title: 'INTERVIEW TRAP: SMOTE on Test Data', text: 'Applying SMOTE before cross-validation leaks synthetic observations into validation folds, yielding artificially inflated performance. Always apply SMOTE inside an imblearn.pipeline.Pipeline.' },
              { title: 'PATTERN: Scikit-Learn ColumnTransformer', text: 'Combine preprocessor = ColumnTransformer(transformers=[(\'num\', num_pipeline, num_cols), (\'cat\', cat_pipeline, cat_cols)]) to encapsulate end-to-end preprocessing.' },
              { title: 'PRODUCTION NOTE: High Cardinality & Unknowns', text: 'Categorical encoders must handle unseen categories at inference time (handle_unknown=\'ignore\' or fallback to global target mean).' }
            ]
